# Which glass has more water?
subtitle: "Water is poured from a short glass into a tall one: tap the glass with more, then hear why young children choose the tall one, as Piaget found."

## Which glass has more water?
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: piaget
      title: La genèse du nombre chez l'enfant (The Child's Conception of Number)
      authors: J. Piaget, A. Szeminska
      year: 1941
      finding: in the liquid conservation task, children in the preoperational stage (about 2-7) judge that a taller, thinner glass holds more; most conserve by about 6-7

    path ga points [[80,460],[80,230],[320,230],[320,460]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wa closed #2f6b8f points [[84,233],[316,233],[316,350],[84,350]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    path gb points [[380,460],[380,230],[620,230],[620,460]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wb closed #2f6b8f points [[384,233],[616,233],[616,350],[384,350]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    path gc points [[690,640],[690,230],[790,230],[790,640]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wc closed #2f6b8f points [[694,233],[786,233],[786,518],[694,518]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    group glass_a members ["ga","wa"] hidden true
    group glass_b members ["gb","wb"] hidden true
    group glass_c members ["gc","wc"] hidden true
    arrow pour #2f6b8f from.x 540 from.y 500 to.x 670 to.y 610 hidden true
    box same "Same" x 790 y 420 width 150 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st_same "same water" #b5482e x 790 y 600 font_size 48 hidden true
    text st_age "most get it by ~7" #2f6b8f cites ["piaget"] x 790 y 520 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Which glass has more water?"

A: Which glass has more water? Start with two identical glasses, filled to exactly the same level.
    draw glass_a glass_b

    draw pour

    erase wb

A: Now I pour this one into a tall, thin glass. Not a drop spilled.
    draw glass_c

    erase pour gb

    move glass_c by [-240,0] duration 1

B: Easy. The tall one, obviously. Look how high the water is.
    draw same

    ask "Which glass has more water now? Tap the glass, or tap Same." choose ["glass_a","glass_c","same"] answer same store c
        right: The same. Nothing was added or spilled; the water only changed shape.
        wrong: Think back to the pouring.

    erase same

B: Hang on. I said the tall one.
    draw st_same

A: So do most four-year-olds. They point to the tall glass, because the water is higher.
    highlight glass_c

B: Even after watching it being poured?
    highlight wc

A: Even then. The Swiss psychologist Jean Piaget made this little test famous.
    text st_pia "Piaget, 1941" #2f6b8f cites ["piaget"] x 790 y 440 font_size 36

A: He called it conservation: knowing that an amount stays the same when its shape changes. Most children get it at around six or seven.
    draw st_age

B: So that's why my little brother always grabbed the tall glass.
    highlight glass_c

A: Smart move, though: same juice, bigger-looking prize.
    highlight st_same effect circle
---
